1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:07,150
Two beams meet end to end, each
cut away to about half its width.

2
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:12,080
The upper piece is lowered
from above, and two

3
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:14,950
hardwood pegs are driven
in from the sides.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:18,343
This is the okkake daisen-tsugi.

5
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:25,310
One English glossary describes it as an
oblique, housed and rabbeted scarf joint.

6
00:00:30,767 --> 00:00:35,697
Japanese references read
it okkake daisen-tsugi.

7
00:00:36,607 --> 00:00:41,197
Daisen is written with the
characters for large and peg.

8
00:00:42,147 --> 00:00:45,600
Without the pegs, the same
joint is called okkake-tsugi.

9
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:52,547
In episode nine, the Flat 35
house specification offered two

10
00:00:52,587 --> 00:00:57,197
splices for the beams in the
outer walls at second-floor level:

11
00:00:57,667 --> 00:00:59,917
the gooseneck, or this one.

12
00:01:03,467 --> 00:01:07,717
Unlike the gooseneck, the two
pieces aren't different shapes.

13
00:01:08,327 --> 00:01:13,247
Both are the same shape, turned
end for end, though one training

14
00:01:13,287 --> 00:01:17,937
course warns that the slope runs
the other way on the upper piece.

15
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:22,137
They're called the lower
piece and the upper piece:

16
00:01:22,627 --> 00:01:27,417
the lower piece is set first, and
the upper piece is lowered onto it.

17
00:01:27,867 --> 00:01:34,577
Over the splice, each piece keeps about
half the width, but its full depth.

18
00:01:35,507 --> 00:01:41,447
Seen from above, the joint line runs
along the beam, with a step at the

19
00:01:41,487 --> 00:01:47,297
middle, the ago, and a small
tongue at each end, the mechigai.

20
00:01:47,567 --> 00:01:50,797
The step at the middle has a slight slope.

21
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:55,947
As the upper piece is lowered,
the slope draws the two pieces

22
00:01:55,987 --> 00:02:01,217
together along the beam, until
the end shoulders close up tight.

23
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:06,670
Then two pegs go in through the
side faces, from opposite sides.

24
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:09,790
They stop the joint lifting back out.

25
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:15,510
It's the slope, not the pegs, that the
sources credit with drawing it tight.

26
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:19,640
In our model, without the
pegs it can't be pulled apart

27
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:24,330
or pushed sideways, but
it can lift straight out.

28
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:27,130
With the pegs in, it can't.

29
00:02:31,167 --> 00:02:37,927
The Flat 35 specification offers
it for those outer-wall beams, for

30
00:02:37,967 --> 00:02:43,377
eaves plates, and for one option
on second-floor floor beams.

31
00:02:44,227 --> 00:02:48,187
For roof beams it's the first
choice; the gooseneck is

32
00:02:48,227 --> 00:02:51,907
allowed only where the beam
is about a hundred and twenty

33
00:02:51,947 --> 00:02:56,677
millimeters deep, and then
with steel straps and bolts.

34
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:00,920
None of those clauses names
hardware for this splice.

35
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:07,930
As in each of them, it's placed off the
post, a short way out along the beam.

36
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:13,657
How long should it be?

37
00:03:14,307 --> 00:03:18,867
A carpenters' training course says
about three times the depth gives the

38
00:03:18,907 --> 00:03:24,893
most strength, and a joinery handbook
gives three to four times for beams

39
00:03:24,933 --> 00:03:28,837
a hundred and five or a hundred
and twenty millimeters deep.

40
00:03:29,907 --> 00:03:34,457
One test database suggests
one and a half to three times.

41
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:38,890
Our model uses one
laboratory's test proportions:

42
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:43,260
a beam a hundred and twenty
millimeters wide and a

43
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:47,470
hundred and eighty deep, with
a splice twice the depth.

44
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:54,353
In that lab's tests, at the same depth,
these splices held two to three and a half

45
00:03:54,393 --> 00:04:00,143
times the pull of its gooseneck specimens,
though they were also much longer.

46
00:04:01,013 --> 00:04:06,503
Again, the depth of the beam mattered
more than the length of the splice.

47
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:12,337
It resists bending better than
the gooseneck, but only partly.

48
00:04:13,047 --> 00:04:17,957
The test database notes it's
often treated as a pin in design.

49
00:04:18,367 --> 00:04:24,857
And it's slow to cut by hand, because the
two pieces are fitted against each other.

50
00:04:25,247 --> 00:04:31,377
One architect's guide reports one
to four splices per person per day.

51
00:04:35,567 --> 00:04:36,657
Next time:

52
00:04:36,987 --> 00:04:39,517
marking it out and cutting it.

53
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:42,730
The okkake daisen-tsugi:

54
00:04:43,313 --> 00:04:48,573
two pieces of the same shape,
the upper lowered onto the lower

55
00:04:48,613 --> 00:04:52,823
along a slope, then pegged
so they can't lift apart.
